  "numbers": [
    "Digest, Appendix C: \"banh mi\" used by 36 cluster members over 7 days, 3.91 uses per 1,000 cluster posts against 0.16 in the three weeks before (ratio 20.16); \"bahn mi\" (new) by 11 members over 4 days. The scope review notes baselines are rough this week: do not put the ratio on screen as a fact without a check.",
    "My count from posts.jsonl (any of the spellings \"banh mi\", \"bahn mi\", \"bánh mì\", case-insensitive, 13 Nov 00:00Z to 19 Nov 09:54Z): 134 posts by 85 accounts, 46 of them cluster members, on all seven days."
  ],
  "couldBeShown": "Two posts carry described images (a bowl of tofu phở; a \"self proclaimed free thinkers\" meme). One is a video with no description in the data (\"pronouncing bluesky like banh mi\").",
  "sensitive": "Where the banh mi wave came from is not for the show. Other posts in the window by some of the same accounts frame it around trans women, and a thread about it turns to hormone medication and nudes (R10: gender identity, health, sexuality). The digest flags several of those posts one by one, and flags the whole thread \"why is everyone banh mi posting?\" (story 12, which also holds the \"banhmi-contagion\" graph the build bot made): nothing from that thread is on this card and the check refuses it. None of this should be used as explanation or background; the show cannot say why the wave started beyond what the posts listed here say. On this card specifically: (1) @alpacasoft.dev followed his meme with a reply about his family that the digest flags (health); it is left out and the meme should not be tied to it. (2) @pixeldreams.tokyo is a small account (267 followers): here only as someone happily joining in (R7). (3) @fasterthanli.me (17,316 followers), @asap.systems and their posts are from outside the cluster's membership; @eugenevinitsky.bsky.social (15,020), @eva.computer (18,063) and @words.bsky.social (10,258) are members with large followings (see the scope review on large accounts). (4) @elucidating.extradimensional.space leads a story the digest flags (story 18) and his other posts this week are mostly about it; this post is unrelated. (5) \"am I shadow banh mi\" puns on \"shadow ban\", and @eugenevinitsky's post is about propaganda on decentralised social media: both are about the sandwich wave, but the week also had a moderation dispute the digest flags (stories 25, 27), and that connection should be left alone. (6) Left off the card: posts tying the wave to appearance (\"hot women\", \"beautiful women\"), a much-liked pun on being banned from the site by a non-member, and posts about what a banh mi costs the poster."
}
